# Deliberately empty of rules. Do not add the SPA catch-all here.
#
# Cloudflare Pages already does the SPA fallback, and does it correctly:
#   "If your project does not include a top-level 404.html file, Pages assumes that you
#    are deploying a single-page application. ... Pages' default single-page application
#    behavior matches all incoming paths to the root (/), allowing you to capture URLs
#    like /about or /help and respond to them from within your SPA."
#   developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/serving-pages/, read 2026-09-07
# The build writes exactly one top-level HTML file, index.html, and no 404.html, so this
# applies. Check with: find app/dist -maxdepth 1 -name '*.html'
#
# The rule a Vercel port reaches for, "/* /index.html 200", is actively wrong here,
# and the reason is a difference between the two platforms rather than a syntax gap.
#
# Cloudflare consults _redirects before it looks for the asset:
#   "Redirects are always followed, regardless of whether or not an asset matches the
#    incoming request."
#   developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/redirects/, read 2026-09-07
#
# Vercel checks the filesystem first. Its routing runs in phases, and the Build Output
# API documents handle: 'filesystem' as "check matches after the filesystem misses",
# with handle: 'rewrite' later still, so a vercel.json rewrite never sees a request that
# an existing file answers.
#   vercel.com/docs/build-output-api/configuration, read 2026-09-07
#
# So on Vercel the /((?!assets/).*) lookahead is belt and braces; here there is no
# filesystem check to fall back on, and /* would proxy every request for
# /assets/index-<hash>.js to index.html, serving HTML where the browser expects
# JavaScript. _redirects also has no negative lookahead to copy the exclusion with:
# splats and placeholders only.
#
# Other facts from the same page, so nobody has to re-derive them:
#   - 2,000 static and 100 dynamic rules, 2,100 combined, 1,000 characters per rule.
#   - Order matters; the top-most rule for a source wins; static before dynamic.
#   - 200 is supported, as "Proxying", relative URLs only. Other rewrite codes are not.
#   - Only the first proxy applies: /a -> /b and /b -> /c does not make /a serve /c.
#   - Redirects run before headers, so a request matching both lets the redirect win.
